## Verify Configuration Before Boot

Before booting compute nodes, verify that boot-service and metadata-service are
configured correctly. These checks catch the most common failures before a
wasted boot cycle.

First, confirm that boot-service has the correct kernel parameters stored:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify boot-service boot config is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k boot config list -F yaml \
	| grep -q "ds=nocloud-net" \
	&& echo "boot-service: OK" \
	|| { echo "boot-service: FAILED - check boot config"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the cloud-init default configuration was uploaded
successfully:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init defaults are uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata defaults list -F yaml \
	| grep -q "base_url" \
	&& echo "cloud-init defaults: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init defaults: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the compute group cloud-init configuration is present.
This is what delivers the SSH keys and munge key to the node at boot:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init compute group is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata group list -F yaml \
	| grep -qE "^ +name: compute$" \
	&& echo "cloud-init group: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init group: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, confirm that the metadata-service knows each compute node. This is the
lookup the node itself performs at boot, and the only check here that has to
run after node discovery.

The metadata-service does not ask SMD when a node boots. It answers from its
own copy of SMD's Ethernet interfaces, which it refreshes every 60 seconds by
default (`--smd-sync-interval`), so a node registered a moment ago is not known
to it yet. For an address it does not know, it still answers `200` on
`/metadata-service/meta-data`, with the identity of another component. A node
that gets this has no root SSH key and no munge key, and it never becomes
reachable. So check what it returns, not the status: wait until the node's
meta-data names the node's own xname before powering the nodes on.

The check below asks the metadata-service the question the node will ask, with
`X-Forwarded-For` carrying the node's address so that the server resolves the
same entry. It asks the server's container directly rather than through
haproxy, because haproxy replaces `X-Forwarded-For` with the address the
request came from, so through it the server would look up the head node
instead. The node's own request through haproxy is tested when it boots.
The container's address exists only on the head node, so the check tells
curl to ignore an `http_proxy` set in the environment; a proxy cannot reach
the podman network and the request would time out there.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify the metadata-service knows each compute node -->
```bash
metadata_ip=$(podman inspect --format \
	'{%raw%}{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}{%endraw%}' \
	metadata-service)
for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)); do
	for ((t=0; ; t++)) ; do
		curl -fsS -m 5 --noproxy '*' \
			-H "X-Forwarded-For: ${c_ip[$i]}" \
			"http://${metadata_ip}:8080/meta-data" \
			| grep -qx "instance-id: x1000c0s0b$((i+1))n0" && break
		(( t < 18 )) || {
			echo "metadata-service node: FAILED for ${c_name[$i]}"
			exit 1
		}
		echo "Waiting for the metadata-service to know ${c_name[$i]}..."
		sleep 5
	done
done
echo "metadata-service node: OK"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
